.TH OPENALLOYD 1 "October 2026" "OpenAlloy 1.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
openalloyd \- OpenAlloy lighting service for the HyperX Alloy Origins Core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openalloyd
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-simulate | \-\-simulate\-hp ]
.RB [ \-\-sim\-layout " " ansi | abnt2 | iso ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openalloyd
keeps the lighting of a HyperX Alloy Origins Core keyboard running for the
logged\-in user.
It is normally started by the systemd
.B user
service
.I openalloy.service
that
.B install.sh
installs; it never runs as root.
.PP
The service finds the keyboard by USB ID and interface, chooses the vendor
channel (interface 2) or HID LampArray (interface 3), and works in one of three
modes:
.TP
.B live
Effects are rendered and sent continuously (default 30 frames per second).
Unchanged frames are only refreshed so that the firmware does not fall back to
its onboard profile.
.TP
.B fixed
A color is sent once and the keyboard is left alone (HID LampArray).
The service only watches the keyboard and sends the color again after an
unplug, a reboot or a system resume.
.TP
.B onboard
The keyboard firmware shows its own profile.
.PP
The service reconnects by itself when the keyboard is replugged, detects a
system resume by comparing
.B CLOCK_BOOTTIME
and
.B CLOCK_MONOTONIC
(so it works even while systemd freezes user services during sleep hooks), and
restores the saved lighting.
.PP
.BR openalloy (1)
and its graphical interface talk to the service through a Unix socket with a
line\-based JSON protocol; changes apply at once, without restarting.
The socket is private to the user, and requests are bounded in size and number.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Socket path (default:
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/openalloy.sock ).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More detailed log.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Use a simulated keyboard (vendor channel, firmware 21.08).
.TP
.B \-\-simulate\-hp
Simulate the HP 03f0:098f with firmware 21.16 (HID LampArray only).
.TP
.BR \-\-sim\-layout " " ansi | abnt2 | iso
Layout of the simulated keyboard (default
.BR abnt2 ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Hand the LEDs back to the firmware where appropriate, close the keyboard and
exit.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the settings file.
.SH SYSTEM SLEEP
The hook
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system\-sleep/openalloy
(or
.I /etc/systemd/system\-sleep/openalloy
on read\-only
.IR /usr )
resets the keyboard's USB device before suspend so that the firmware restarts
on its onboard profile; a keyboard left in direct mode without frames may stop
waking the computer.
When user services are not frozen it also tells each running service, with a
short timeout.
The hook never makes the sleep fail.
.SH LOGGING
Messages go to standard error and, under systemd, to the journal, always in
English:
.PP
.RS
.B journalctl \-\-user \-u openalloy \-f
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENALLOY_ENABLE_FLASH
Set to
.B I_ACCEPT_THE_RISK
to unlock onboard writes without the live RGB test.
.TP
.B OPENALLOY_SOCKET
Socket path when
.B \-\-socket
is not given.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Locations of the settings and of the socket.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/openalloy.service
The user service.
.TP
.I ~/.config/openalloy/config.json
Settings, shared with
.BR openalloy (1).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/openalloy.sock
Control socket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
systemctl \-\-user status openalloy
systemctl \-\-user restart openalloy
openalloyd \-\-simulate\-hp \-v      # try it without the keyboard
.fi
